{"st":"SC","name":"South Carolina","seats":[{"census":1789,"seats":5},{"census":1790,"seats":6},{"census":1800,"seats":8},{"census":1810,"seats":9},{"census":1820,"seats":9},{"census":1830,"seats":9},{"census":1840,"seats":7},{"census":1850,"seats":6},{"census":1860,"seats":4},{"census":1870,"seats":5},{"census":1880,"seats":7},{"census":1890,"seats":7},{"census":1900,"seats":7},{"census":1910,"seats":7},{"census":1920,"seats":7},{"census":1930,"seats":6},{"census":1940,"seats":6},{"census":1950,"seats":6},{"census":1960,"seats":6},{"census":1970,"seats":6},{"census":1980,"seats":6},{"census":1990,"seats":6},{"census":2000,"seats":6},{"census":2010,"seats":7},{"census":2020,"seats":7}],"events":[{"congress":1,"year":1788,"kind":"statehood","title":"Ratified the Constitution","text":"South Carolina ratified the Constitution on May 23, 1788, the 8th state to do so. The Constitution gave it 5 seats in the House until the first census.","sources":["https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/R47747.html","https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":3,"year":1792,"kind":"census","title":"1790 census apportionment","text":"After the 1790 census, South Carolina's House delegation grew from 5 to 6 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":8,"year":1802,"kind":"census","title":"1800 census apportionment","text":"After the 1800 census, South Carolina's House delegation grew from 6 to 8 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":13,"year":1812,"kind":"census","title":"1810 census apportionment","text":"After the 1810 census, South Carolina's House delegation grew from 8 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":18,"year":1822,"kind":"census","title":"1820 census apportionment","text":"After the 1820 census, South Carolina kept 9 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":23,"year":1832,"kind":"census","title":"1830 census apportionment","text":"After the 1830 census, South Carolina kept 9 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":28,"year":1842,"kind":"census","title":"1840 census apportionment","text":"After the 1840 census, South Carolina's House delegation shrank from 9 to 7 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":33,"year":1852,"kind":"census","title":"1850 census apportionment","text":"After the 1850 census, South Carolina's House delegation shrank from 7 to 6 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":38,"year":1862,"kind":"census","title":"1860 census apportionment","text":"After the 1860 census, South Carolina's House delegation shrank from 6 to 4 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"census","title":"1870 census apportionment","text":"After the 1870 census, South Carolina's House delegation grew from 4 to 5 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"at-large","title":"Some members elected at-large","text":"In the 43rd Congress (1873–1875), South Carolina elected one of its representatives statewide (at-large) and the rest by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":48,"year":1882,"kind":"census","title":"1880 census apportionment","text":"After the 1880 census, South Carolina's House delegation grew from 5 to 7 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":53,"year":1892,"kind":"census","title":"1890 census apportionment","text":"After the 1890 census, South Carolina kept 7 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":58,"year":1902,"kind":"census","title":"1900 census apportionment","text":"After the 1900 census, South Carolina kept 7 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"census","title":"1910 census apportionment","text":"After the 1910 census, South Carolina kept 7 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"census","title":"1930 census apportionment","text":"After the 1930 census, South Carolina's House delegation shrank from 7 to 6 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":78,"year":1942,"kind":"census","title":"1940 census apportionment","text":"After the 1940 census, South Carolina kept 6 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":83,"year":1952,"kind":"census","title":"1950 census apportionment","text":"After the 1950 census, South Carolina kept 6 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":88,"year":1962,"kind":"census","title":"1960 census apportionment","text":"After the 1960 census, South Carolina kept 6 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":93,"year":1972,"kind":"census","title":"1970 census apportionment","text":"After the 1970 census, South Carolina kept 6 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":98,"year":1982,"kind":"census","title":"1980 census apportionment","text":"After the 1980 census, South Carolina kept 6 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":98,"year":1982,"kind":"court","title":"Court adopts House-passed plan","text":"After the two legislative chambers deadlocked, a federal court on March 8, 1982, adopted the congressional plan that had passed the state House, finding it the most acceptable plan submitted.","sources":["https://hallapproved.com/sc/cases/federal/district/1982/1507760","https://leagle.com/decision/19821711533fsupp117811564"]},{"congress":103,"year":1992,"kind":"census","title":"1990 census apportionment","text":"After the 1990 census, South Carolina kept 6 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":103,"year":1992,"kind":"court","title":"Burton v. Sheheen","text":"The governor vetoed the state legislative plans and the legislature never passed a congressional plan, so a federal court drew its own congressional map on May 1, 1992. It included one district with a 57.9% Black voting-age population.","sources":["https://hallapproved.com/sc/cases/federal/district/1992/1368984"]},{"congress":108,"year":2002,"kind":"census","title":"2000 census apportionment","text":"After the 2000 census, South Carolina kept 6 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":108,"year":2002,"kind":"court","title":"Colleton County Council v. McConnell","text":"The governor vetoed the legislature's redistricting bill in August 2001 and no later plan passed, so a federal court drew congressional districts on March 20, 2002.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/south-carolina/"]},{"congress":113,"year":2012,"kind":"census","title":"2010 census apportionment","text":"After the 2010 census, South Carolina's House delegation grew from 6 to 7 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":118,"year":2022,"kind":"census","title":"2020 census apportionment","text":"After the 2020 census, South Carolina kept 7 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":118,"year":2024,"kind":"court","title":"Alexander v. South Carolina State Conference of the NAACP","text":"The Supreme Court reversed a lower court's finding that the 1st District was a racial gerrymander, holding that the challengers had not separated the role of race from the role of politics.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-807"]},{"congress":119,"year":2026,"kind":"mid-decade","tit