:["SC-1"],"c088":["SC-1"],"c089":["SC-1"],"c090":["SC-1"],"c091":["SC-1"],"c092":["SC-1"],"c093":["SC-1"],"c094":["SC-1"],"c095":["SC-1"],"c096":["SC-1"],"c097":["SC-1"],"c098":["SC-1"],"c099":["SC-1"],"c100":["SC-1"],"c101":["SC-1"],"c102":["SC-1"],"c108":["SC-2"],"c109":["SC-2"],"c110":["SC-2"],"c111":["SC-2"],"c112":["SC-2"],"c113":["SC-1"],"c114":["SC-1"]},"compactnessNote":"Polsby-Popper compactness (4π × area ÷ perimeter²) measured on the projected district shapes. It describes shape only: coastlines, rivers and county lines lower it too, so a low score is not evidence of intent. \"Irregular\" marks the least compact 5% of district shapes in each census era nationally.","mapChanges":{"c003":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1790 census, which raised South Carolina's seats from 5 to 6. It was first used in the 1792 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":3},"c008":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1800 census, which raised South Carolina's seats from 6 to 8. It was first used in the 1802 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":8},"c013":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1810 census, which raised South Carolina's seats from 8 to 9. It was first used in the 1812 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":8},"c015":{"why":"small","kind":"other","text":"Not a new map: the historical boundary records show only slight differences in one district from the previous Congress, and no statewide redistricting is recorded for this election.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":1},"c018":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1820 census, which left South Carolina with 9 seats. It was first used in the 1822 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":5},"c028":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1840 census, which cut South Carolina's seats from 9 to 7. It was first used in the 1842 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":9},"c033":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1850 census, which cut South Carolina's seats from 7 to 6. It was first used in the 1852 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c037":{"why":"at-large","kind":"other","text":"South Carolina sent no members to the 37th Congress (1861–1863), so the map shows only its outline and no districts.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":0},"c040":{"why":"at-large","kind":"other","text":"From the 40th Congress the historical boundary records again show districts for South Carolina, after Congresses in which it sent no members.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":4},"c043":{"why":"at-large","kind":"at-large","text":"After the 1870 census raised South Carolina's seats from 4 to 5, the state kept its existing districts and elected one member statewide (at-large).","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators"],"districtsChanged":0},"c044":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"South Carolina's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1874 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":5},"c045":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"South Carolina's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1876 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":2},"c048":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1880 census, which raised South Carolina's seats from 5 to 7. It was first used in the 1882 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c054":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1890 census, which left South Carolina with 7 seats. It was first used in the 1894 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":3},"c056":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"South Carolina's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1898 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":6},"c058":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1900 census, which left South Carolina with 7 seats. It was first used in the 1902 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":6},"c065":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"South Carolina's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1916 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":6},"c073":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1930 census, which cut South Carolina's seats from 7 to 6. It was first used in the 1932 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":5},"c089":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1960 census, which left South Carolina with 6 seats. It was first used in the 1964 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":2},"c090":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"South Carolina's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1966 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":3},"c093":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1970 census, which left South Carolina with 6 seats. It was first used in the 1972 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":5},"c098":{"why":"event","kind":"court","text":"New map following: Court adopts House-passed plan. After the two legislative chambers deadlocked, a federal court on March 8, 1982, adopted the congressional plan that had passed the state House, finding it the most acceptable plan submitted.","sources":["https://hallapproved.com/sc/cases/federal/district/1982/1507760","https://leagle.com/decision/19821711533fsupp117811564"],"districtsChanged":6},"c103":{"why":"event","kind":"court","text":"New map following: Burton v. Sheheen. The governor vetoed the state legislative plans and the legislature never passed a congressional plan, so a federal court drew its own congressional map on May 1, 1992. It included one district with a 57.9% Black voting-age population.","sources":["https://hallapproved.com/sc/cases/federal/district/1992/1368984"],"districtsChanged":6},"c104":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"South Carolina's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1994 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":3},"c108":{"why":"event","kind":"court","text":"New map following: Colleton County Council v. McConnell. The governor vetoed the legislature's redistricting bill in August 2001 and no later plan passed, so a federal court drew congressional districts on March 20, 2002.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/south-carolina/"],"districtsChanged":6},"c113":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 2010 census, which raised South Carolina's seats from 6 to 7. It was first used in the 2012 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c115":{"why":"small","kind":"other","text":"Not a new map: the historical boundary records show only slight differences in 3 districts from the previous Congress, and no statewide redistricting is recorded for this election.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":3},"c118":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 2020 census, which left South Carolina with 7 seats. It was first used in the 2022 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":7}}}